From 18b5106406a7ff0f8df59e2cb4cade9217b0bba9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 19:36:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: lower the fidelity of native Dolby Vision only
 where the GPU cannot keep up

Where the GPU cannot draw a native Dolby Vision frame in the time of a
refresh, even drawn the faster way (CDVPathChoice) and a frame ahead
(CDVAhead), a profile 7 FEL film stutters: an Intel N100 class GPU, a
small AMD iGPU. The fidelity step-down levels of the bridge trade some
precision for GPU time, but graphics that keep up must keep the faithful
picture, and what a level saves is only known once it is measured on the
GPU at hand, where it may be nothing. CDVStepDown (DVStepDown.h, a
header-only class that only decides, as the two it follows) chooses the
level of each picture format from measurements alone, and assumes no
saving:

- Nothing goes down while the two ways of drawing a format are compared,
  nor while frame ahead can still be had and the frames are not
  presented that way yet: the remedies that cost no fidelity come first.
- A window of 48 frames is behind when both the GPU times and the
  refreshes say so: the GPU times that the GPU is the cause, the missed
  refreshes that the frames do not keep up. Presented a frame ahead,
  frames that take more than 85 % of the period on average (without the
  slowest), and 5 % of the presentations late by the times of the flips
  that the kernel tells. Presented the way that waits, 5 % of the frames
  over 80 % of the period (the mark measured on the 7950X3D iGPU) and 5 %
  of the presentations over 1.5 periods. Three at least of each. Left
  out: frames with GUI elements, subtitles or a capture, pictures that
  stand still, the first two seconds of a video and after a seek, and the
  first frames after a change of format, of level or of way.
- Two windows in a row behind, or one whose frames could not keep up in
  any case, take the format down one level of its ladder (the levels
  that change anything for it, as the renderer tells them:
  dvbridge_render_step_levels()); never to a level that changes nothing.
  Each level is measured before anything more is decided. A level
  measured to save nothing (less than 5 %, or no more than twice what the
  two windows could be apart by chance) is passed over; when no level
  keeps up the format goes to the fastest measured, the lowest of those
  that do not differ, and stays: with no level that saves anything, the
  faithful picture.
- Back up by measurement only: when the level above, as the level in use
  takes now times what the two took for each other at their last change,
  fits with room to spare (75 % of the period a frame ahead, 70 % the way
  that waits; the room keeps a format from going up and down). That is
  the case at a longer refresh period, or when the GPU was held up. There
  are no trials after a while: a weak GPU does not stutter now and then
  to look again. Up a minute after going down at the earliest; a level up
  that does not keep up sends the format back down with that
  measurement, and doubles the wait, up to about an hour of film.
- A format is a CDVFrameLoad (size, active area, residual drawn), with a
  level of its own: a lighter one starts faithful. What was found is kept
  from one video to the next, and per GPU, driver and build from one
  session to the next (the hidden setting videoscreen.hadesdvstep, as
  videoscreen.hadesdvpath), with its ladder: the first frames of a FEL
  film are drawn at the level found before. A format whose ladder changes
  begins again from level 0.
- System > Display, "Dolby Vision: lower the fidelity only when the
  graphics cannot keep up" (videoscreen.hadesdvstepdown), on by default;
  off, every frame is drawn at level 0. Spanish in es_es, es_ar and es_mx,
  through Estuary as the other settings of Hades. A level that
  advancedsettings.xml forces (dvstepdown) overrides both, and the policy
  learns nothing meanwhile. The picture rebuilt for output as HDR10 stays
  at level 0.
- The log tells each change once, and why: the figures of the window that
  made it and what each level of the ladder took. The Dolby Vision page of
  the player information shows the level, as before.
- CDVPathChoice gets Comparing(), and Changed(): a change of level drops
  what a load had gathered, and a comparison under way, which would be of
  two levels. CDVFrameLoad gets WithResidual().

A host test plays simulated videos through CDVPathChoice and CDVAhead as
they are and CDVStepDown, with a model of each GPU and of how its frames
miss their refresh, and several assumed savings per level, none among
them: 138 checks. A strong GPU (7 to 18 ms, at 24 and 60 Hz, every mode of
frame ahead, GUI, seeks, a slow first second, refreshes missed for other
reasons) and the 7950X3D iGPU as measured (26.9 ms composed, a frame
ahead) stay at level 0. A weak GPU (50 to 70 ms of 41.7) goes down one
level at a time after the ways were compared, stops at the first level
that keeps up, and does not change again in an hour; where none keeps up
it stays at the fastest, and with no saving it goes back to 0. Ladders
with levels that change nothing, a lighter format, a longer refresh
period, a GPU held up and one whose estimate is wrong, the setting, a
forced level, and what is kept from one session to the next are played
too. 18 mutations of the policy each make it fail. The existing host
tests of CDVPathChoice and CDVAhead pass with the changed header. Not run
on a GPU: the marks for frames presented a frame ahead and what each
level saves are to be seen on one.
